body:
This PR disallows direct access to the `window` object to keep the library SSR-friendly.

## ESLint rule

The `no-restricted-globals` ESLint rule was added to our source files (except our tests and widgets which are running on browser environments). It errors when you access directly `window`:

![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/6137112/138685248-a5e7fa3e-a98f-4d6d-a3c3-562aeeb64a26.png)

## Safely running code on the browser

There's now an internal helper to help us run code safely on browsers:

```ts
safelyRunOnBrowser(({ window }) => {
  // Safely access `window`
});
```

## Safely running code on the server

The `fallback` option is used to run code on the server:

```ts
const location = safelyRunOnBrowser(({ window }) => window.location, {
  fallback: () => {
    throw new Error(
      'You need to provide `getLocation` to the `history` router in environments where `window` does not exist.'
    );
  },
});
```

Note that `safelyRunOnBrowser` returns the value of the callback, which is handy to use as an expression.
